{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>The appointed seasons of YHWH which you shall proclaim as holy convocations.</b> And adjacent to it is the commandment of Shabbat, and Rashi explained: what has Shabbat to do with the appointed seasons, etc. And I say that this is its explanation: for we already explained above in parashat Vayakhel (Exodus 35:2) regarding what Rashi explained — that Shabbat was placed before the work of the Mishkan to tell you that the Mishkan does not override Shabbat — for in every case the commandment that comes first overrides the one that comes later. And this verse, 'which you shall proclaim as holy convocations,' speaks of the fixing of the year and the month and the matter of intercalation. And it is concluded in the Yalkut on this parashah: from where do we know that they profane Shabbat to testify concerning them [the new moon]? Scripture says: 'which you shall proclaim them in their season.' One might think that just as they profane Shabbat to testify concerning them, so too they should profane Shabbat to give notice that they have been confirmed — Scripture says: 'which you shall proclaim,' etc. And therefore the commandment of intercalation was placed before the commandment of Shabbat, to inform that intercalation overrides Shabbat. And after Shabbat he returned and detailed the commandment of intercalation in the verse 'these are the appointed seasons of YHWH,' in order to place the commandment of Shabbat before intercalation, to tell you that intercalation does not override Shabbat — and this one [desecration] is for testifying concerning them, and this one is for giving notice that they have been confirmed. And this law is also in tractate Rosh HaShanah, chapter one." "Another matter: 'which you shall proclaim them in their season' — this proclaiming is through words of Torah, that they should engage in the laws of Pesach on Pesach, etc.; therefore it says 'in their season,' and concerning this proclaiming it says 'these are My appointed seasons.' But when they are not engaged in them but only in eating and drinking, they are not the appointed seasons of YHWH at all, and concerning them it is said (Isaiah 1:14): 'Your new moons and your appointed seasons — My soul hates them.' And what is written, 'these you shall do for YHWH at your appointed seasons' (Numbers 29:39) — is because one must give half to YHWH and half to yourselves."], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {0 "The Kli Yakar uses the term עִבּוּר broadly to encompass both intercalation of months (sanctifying the new moon) and of years; translated consistently as 'intercalation.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:38:02.419604992Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}